@namespace AuthWithAdmin.Client.Components
@*
    MotivaLoaderMark — the animated Motiva icon, and nothing else.

    The only copy of the loader geometry in the Razor tree. It is split
    out from MotivaLoader so that a host which already owns the status
    role and the label — MLoadingState does — can drop the branded mark
    in without nesting a second role="status" or a second visible string
    inside its own. Nobody should re-inline these paths anywhere else.

    ── WHERE THIS GEOMETRY COMES FROM ───────────────────────────────────
    The mark is the "o" of the Motiva wordmark, and the wordmark in
    Client/wwwroot/images/motiva-logo.png is the reference. These paths
    were measured off it: the two coloured halves were isolated, a
    stroked rounded-rectangle model was fitted to each, and the fit
    reproduces the logo at IoU 0.96 (teal) / 0.97 (purple).

    They are NOT the two SVGs in Client/wwwroot/assets/loader/. Those
    were an earlier hand-off and they do not match the logo — fitted
    against the same reference they score IoU 0.54 / 0.61. Concretely
    they carry a stroke 30% too heavy for the mark (0.31 of the
    centreline width against the logo's 0.22), a centreline rectangle
    too wide (1.35 against 1.23) and, once they had been pushed apart
    far enough not to touch, a composed silhouette far too square (1.13
    against 1.18) with about a third of the logo's internal negative
    space. That is why the loader read as two fat opposing C-shapes
    rather than as the Motiva "o". Do not "restore" those files over
    this; if a corrected vector is ever exported, re-measure against the
    wordmark before trusting it.

    ── THE COMPOSITION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Both halves sit on ONE shared centreline rectangle:

        x 0 → 453,  y 0 → 368,  corner radius 152,  stroke 100

    That is the whole trick, and it is what the earlier version got
    wrong. The gap at the two seams is NOT made by moving the halves
    apart — in the logo each arm simply STOPS SHORT of the corner the
    other one owns: 138 units back horizontally, 102 vertically. So

      · teal   runs from (138, 0)   — 138 short of the left edge —
               along the top, round the top-right corner, and down to
               (453, 266), stopping 102 above the bottom edge;
      · purple runs from (315, 368) — 138 short of the right edge —
               along the bottom, round the bottom-left corner, and up
               to (0, 102), stopping 102 below the top edge.

    Pulling the ends back instead of translating the paths is what keeps
    the arms long, the rectangle upright and the negative space open,
    while still leaving the two ends 171.6 apart — a clear 71.6 units of
    white at rest and 66.6 at the animated peak, i.e. two thirds of a
    stroke. The two halves therefore never touch at any frame, which is
    a hard requirement of the mark. There are deliberately NO transforms
    on these paths: an offset would move an arm off the shared rectangle
    and break the alignment that makes the two halves read as one
    symbol.

    Both halves are 515.8 units long — identical, because the pull-backs
    are symmetric — so the draw reads at the same speed in each colour.
    pathLength="1000" normalises them anyway, which is what lets one
    stroke-dasharray/offset rule in motiva-loader.css drive both.

    viewBox is the exact ink bounds at the animated peak (stroke 105, so
    52.5 of overhang on each side) — no padding, no dead space, so the
    mark fills the box a caller sizes. Keep aspect-ratio in
    motiva-loader.css in step with it.

    Purple is second in document order, matching the wordmark's own
    stacking. The halves no longer overlap anywhere, so nothing depends
    on it — but index.html repeats this order, so leave them in step.

    Decorative by design: aria-hidden. The announcement belongs to
    whichever component wraps this one.
*@
<svg class="motiva-loader-mark @Class"
     viewBox="-52.5 -52.5 558 473"
     fill="none"
     xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
     aria-hidden="true"
     focusable="false">
    <path class="motiva-loader-path motiva-loader-blue"
          pathLength="1000"
          d="M138 0L301 0A152 152 0 0 1 453 152L453 266" />
    <path class="motiva-loader-path motiva-loader-purple"
          pathLength="1000"
          d="M315 368L152 368A152 152 0 0 1 0 216L0 102" />
</svg>

@code {
    /// <summary>Extra classes on the &lt;svg&gt;. Sizing is normally set by the
    /// size modifier on the wrapping .motiva-loader, so this is for the rarer
    /// case of a host that sizes the mark directly.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }
}
